Setting a TTL just means computing and storing an absolute expiry time; checking it lazily on read costs one comparison per `get`.

```python
class Store:
    def __init__(self):
        self.data = {}   # key -> Entry(value, expires_at)

    def set(self, key, value, ttl=None):
        expires_at = time.time() + ttl if ttl else None
        self.data[key] = Entry(value, expires_at)

    def get(self, key):
        entry = self.data.get(key)
        if entry is None:
            return None
        if entry.expires_at is not None and time.time() >= entry.expires_at:
            del self.data[key]      # lazy expiry: clean up on the way out
            return None
        return entry.value
```

`ttl=None` means "never expires," so the same `Entry` shape serves both TTL and permanent keys without a separate code path.
